---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="workshop day — tiny soft computer"
  description="What attendees actually do during the tiny soft computer workshop."
  active="workshop"
>

  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 2.5rem;">
    <p class="kicker">workshop day</p>
    <h1>your kit is ready. you're just here to make it yours.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      every kit arrives fully preloaded, CircuitPython, libraries, and
      code are already installed and tested. you won't be doing any of
      the technical setup, that part is already done. here's what you
      will do.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="tag">step one</span>
    <h2>tweak the code</h2>
    <p>
      open <a href="/code">the code</a> and find the list near the top,
      clearly marked. add your own lines, remove ones that don't feel
      right, reorder them. that's the whole interface. keep each one
      short, the screen is small.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="tag">step two</span>
    <h2>wire the button</h2>
    <p>
      your kit includes a small button and two wires. one wire connects
      to pin <code>A2</code>, the other to any <code>GND</code> pin. no
      soldering, no resistors, just two connections.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      a button has 4 legs in 2 connected pairs. your two wires need to be
      on opposite sides. if the button doesn't respond, or responds
      backward, try moving one wire to a different leg.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="tag">step three</span>
    <h2>build your enclosure</h2>
    <p>
      this is the open-ended part. see <a href="/fabrication">fabrication</a>
      for materials and a starting technique, but the form is yours to
      decide. soft, hard, exposed, hidden, anything that fits your
      electronics and lets the screen and button stay accessible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="divider">✿</div>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>a quick note on patience</h2>
    <p>
      the display can only redraw itself once every 180 seconds
      (3 minutes), that's the manufacturer's own spec for this exact
      panel, not something broken. press the button once, then let it
      be. more on why in <a href="/about">about</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

</Layout>